ceos are the highest paid people in the  world period but unlike most of us who  take our salaries and cash ceos  generally receive the vast majority of  their compensation in stock for example  in 2020 tim cook was paid 281 million  dollars but only three million of this  was cash this means that only about one  percent of tim's compensation was in  cash now that's already quite extreme in  comparison to us average people but  founder ceos generally take the strategy  to a whole another level  everyone from elon musk and steve jobs  stop mark zuckerberg larry page and  sergey brin have all taken one dollar  cash salaries in the past this isn't to  say that they weren't paid at all though  they often receive hundreds of millions  if not billions of dollars worth of  stock elon musk for example recently  unlocked a 23 billion compensation tier  even on the years that these guys don't  get a stock compensation they're  compensated in the form of travel  security housing and so on but what's  even the point of this why take a one  dollar salary  one dollar salaries aren't a new  phenomenon by any means in fact they've  been around since the early 1900s but  the first time they gained significant  prominence was during world war ii  unlike the one dollar ceos today who  received extraordinary amounts of  compensation through other avenues the  original one dollar ceos actually only  earned one dollar well kind of  you see it during the height of world  war ii the us government was struggling  to recruit taunted individuals much of  the young talent was sent to the war  while the others preferred to record  private companies so a few prominent  ceos decided to help out the government  for free and set an example this  included executives like philip reid the  ceo of ge and william not senate the ceo  of gm these guys were basically  volunteering for the government but the  law prevents the government from hiring  unpaid volunteers so they of course  decided to pay them out one dollar per  year and these guys became known as the  dollar a year men it should be noted  though that these guys were still being  paid their regular salaries from their  companies so it's not like the total  compensation was only one dollar  but their work at the government  specifically was truly only awarded one  dollar by the end of the war the u.s  employed a one thousand dollar minute  and these guys served in all sorts of  positions william nutson for example  started off in the national defense  advisory commission before becoming  lieutenant general and then the director  of production william was an invaluable  resource in this role because his  knowledge of running efficient factories  and scaling up production were unmatched  before the u.s joined the war they were  producing just 3 000 aircraft per year  but by the end of the war they are  producing 300 000 aircrafts per year  cargo and navy ship production also went  through the roof which helped the war  effort astronomically after the war  ended though most of these guys returned  to their original jobs full time or they  retired and most people forgot about the  one dollar salary up until 1979. at the  time the automotive ministry had been  devastated by the oil crisis and the  company that was hit the hardest was  chrysler things got so bad that chrysler  needed a bailout from the government and  to show that the company was serious  about spending the bill of money  effectively chrysler ceo lee iacocca  decided to take a one dollar salary if  you're not familiar with this man he's  an absolute icon within the automotive  industry and he made one dollar salaries  the default pr move for wealthy ceos the  next prominent example of the one dollar  salary is steve jobs when steve joined  apple in 1996 the company was on the  brink of bankruptcy so it's no wonder he  settled for one dollar salary especially  given that he was already a billionaire  thanks to pixar and as the dot-com  bubble burst we saw dozens of ceos  follow his lead but this was really the  last time that the 1 salary was truly a  sacrifice  since then the one dollar salary has  become more of a strategy than a  sacrifice  one of the greatest perks of taking a  one dollar cash salary is that you can  receive significantly larger  compensation through other avenues while  also minimizing public scrutiny as you  might have already guessed on average  one dollar ceos are paid significantly  more than regular ceos on average one  dollar ceos do earn 623 dollars less in  salary nine hundred forty one thousand  dollars less in bonus and one hundred  and seventeen thousand dollars less in  other forms of cash compensation this  adds up to a total of one point six  eight million dollars less in cash  compensation however on average they  also earn 515 000 more in options 1.5  million more in stock and 1.5 million  dollars more in other forms of non-cash  compensation this adds up to an extra  3.5 million dollars worth of non-cash  compensation this means that the average  one dollar ceo earns 1.87 million more  than the average ceo in terms of total  compensation not only does one dollar  cash salaries help these ceos minimize  public scrutiny but it's the main reason  that they're able to earn so much in the  first place here's the thing many of the  biggest companies in the world run at  little to no profit so they can't afford  to pay massive cash salaries take tesla  for example it wasn't till 2020 that  tesla posted their first full year of  profitability do you really think that  tesla could afford to pay elon a few  hundred million dollars per year much  less 23 billion dollars of course not  even now tesla only has 17.5 billion  dollars in cash so if they were to pay  elon 23 billion dollars they would  literally burn through their entire cash  reserves even paying him a few billion  in cash would be an extremely  irresponsible move but if we're talking  about options or stock that's actually  doable here's the thing when companies  give employees and executives talk  compensation they don't actually pay for  the stock it's not like the company buys  the shares from the stock market and  then awards them to the employees rather  companies are allowed to create shares  out of daenerys as long as that advised  by their employee or executive  compensation plans now just because the  companies creates the shares out of  denir doesn't mean that the stock is  free  rather all of the existing shareholders  of the company are basically agreeing to  dilute their own stakes to pay for this  talk compensation for example when elon  was paid 23 billion dollars tesla's  valuation hovered at around 1 trillion  so shareholders had to dilute their  stakes by about 2.3 percent for elon to  get paid such a massive amount but  that's an extreme example even elon  doesn't usually get paid tens of  billions usually his pay is closer to  something like 500 million and compared  to tesla's big market cap that's only  0.05 dilution as you can see stock  compensation allows companies to offer  extraordinary salaries without  compromising their own balance sheets  and if you think about it it's truly  quite brilliant but what it's not is a  sacrifice  aside from earning a far greater salary  many speculate that one dollar salaries  allow ceos to get away with a much  smaller tax bill the idea is that  executives don't have to pay taxes at  all until they sell  so they'll only have to pay capital  gains taxes as opposed to ordinary  income tax which is a lot higher but  this isn't actually true  executives do have to pay ordinary  income tax when they exercise their  stock options and given the magnitude of  their salaries they usually end up  paying 40 50 in income tax elon for  example had to pay 11 billion in taxes  last year now elon cleverly made it seem  like he was selling stock because people  wanted him to but really he had to you  see it elon had stock options that  translated to 22.8 million shares of  tesla and all of these options were  expiring in august meaning that he had  to exercise or sell these options by  august whenever you exercise or sell  options though it counts as an ordinary  income event in the given that each year  of tesla was worth over a thousand  dollars at the time exercising 22.8  million shares was equivalent to 23.5  billion worth of ordinary income  at a 40 tax rate this translates to 10.7  billion in taxes there's really no way  of getting around this and elon only had  until august to exercise these stock  options so he decided to sell while  tesla was near its all-time highs and he  also made it seem like he was doing his  civic duty when really he was forced to  pay a large tax bill if he wanted to  exercise his options now that elon has  paid ordinary income tax on this stock  he can hold for a long time and he'd  only have to pay capital gains taxes in  the future but this isn't really a tax  advantage if he was paid 23.5 billion  cash it wouldn't have really changed  anything he would have still had to pay  ordinary income tax on the initial  payment and he could have still  benefited from long-term capital gains  tax if he invested the cash the only  time that you can avoid ordinary income  tax altogether is when you get equity  from founding a company and the original  value of your shares is zero thus the  ordinary income tax on those shares is  also zero and you only have to pay  capital gains tax whenever you're paid  with stock that actually has a value  though you do have to pay ordinary  income tax now some of you guys who earn  stock compensation may be saying that  you didn't have to pay ordinary income  tax on your stocks and the likely reason  for this is that your stock was awarded  in the form of isos or incentive stock  options isos are special stock options  that are only tax capital gains but the  only problem with isos is they're capped  at 100 000 per year so this benefit  isn't really applicable to the  executives that we're talking about and  the idea that one dollar ceos can just  avoid ordinary income tax altogether is  simply not true  while one dollar ceos may not be able to  get a tax break by taking their  compensations in stock they do get one  more massive advantage which is  stakeholder morale there's very few  things that give shareholders more  confidence than knowing that the ceo is  all in  and most ceos are more than all in and  given that they borrow money on their  stakes to actually fund their lives this  means that if the underlying stock went  to zero the ceo would be screwed as they  would literally lose 99.9 percent of  their wealth take elon for example  bloomberg estimates that he only has  three billion cash even after selling  tens of billions of dollars worth of  stock  this means that before he started  selling tesla late last year he likely  had less than one billion dollars in  cash  now this is not a bad amount by any  means and it's not like he's going to go  homeless if his companies collapse but i  don't know about you but i wouldn't want  to go from 300 billion to 300 million  overnight and this is likely extremely  strong motivation to not let any of his  companies go bankrupt this isn't as big  of a concern when we're talking about  mature companies with solid cash flow  but for startups ceos taking one dollar  salaries is a strong commitment for  example amazon stock crashed 95 percent  after the dot-com bubble jeff bezos  being all in during this time period was  no doubt quite a statement at the end of  the day one dollar salaries came about  due to times of hardship but over time  they've evolved into a marketing ploy  and a strategy for earning bigger  compensations they don't have a bunch of  tax advantages like many assume but it  does do one job better than anything  else when a ceo takes all of their  salary in stock they don't get paid  online shareholders also get paid and  thus far there has been no greater  symbol of allegiance and loyalty to  shareholders than taking a one dollar  salary would you be willing to take your  entire salary in stock comment that down  below  also drop a like if you thought this  video explained one dollar salaries well  and of course consider checking out our  discord community to suggest future  video ideas and consider subscribing to  see more questions logically answered  but until then i'm hardy i'll see you  guys on the next one
 

